::GEO_U06_L02_GP01::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U06_L02_GP01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CIR.9<br><br>Question:<br>What is the equation of a circle with center (2, -3) and radius 4? {
=A. (x - 2)&sup2; + (y + 3)&sup2; = 16#The signs match (x - h) and (y - k), and r&sup2; = 16.
~B. (x + 2)&sup2; + (y - 3)&sup2; = 16#This reverses the center signs.
~C. (x - 2)&sup2; + (y + 3)&sup2; = 4#The right side should be r&sup2;, not r.
~D. (x + 3)&sup2; + (y - 2)&sup2; = 16#This switches the coordinate values.
}

::GEO_U06_L02_GP02::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U06_L02_GP02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CIR.10<br><br>Question:<br>The equation (x + 1)&sup2; + (y - 5)&sup2; = 9 has what center and radius? {
~A. Center (1, -5), radius 9#The signs are reversed and the radius is not 9.
=B. Center (-1, 5), radius 3#The center is (-1, 5), and the radius is the square root of 9.
~C. Center (-1, 5), radius 9#The center is correct, but the radius is 3.
~D. Center (1, -5), radius 3#The radius is correct, but the center signs are reversed.
}

::GEO_U06_L02_GP03::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U06_L02_GP03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.9<br><br>Question:<br>Which coordinate tool finds the radius when the center and a point on the circle are known? {
~A. Slope formula#Slope gives direction, not distance from center to circle.
~B. Midpoint formula#Midpoint finds a halfway point, not the radius.
=C. Distance formula#The radius is the distance from the center to a point on the circle.
~D. Area formula#Area is not used to find coordinate distance.
}

::GEO_U06_L02_GP04::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U06_L02_GP04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CIR.9<br><br>Question:<br>A circle has diameter endpoints (-2, 4) and (6, 4). What is its center? {
~A. (-2, 4)#That is one endpoint, not the midpoint.
~B. (6, 4)#That is one endpoint, not the midpoint.
~C. (2, 8)#The x-coordinate is averaged correctly, but the y-coordinate is not.
=D. (2, 4)#The center is the midpoint of the diameter endpoints.
}

::GEO_U06_L02_GP05::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U06_L02_GP05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CIR.10<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement correctly interprets (x - 6)&sup2; + (y + 2)&sup2; = 25 in a map model? {
=A. The center is (6, -2), and points on the circle are 5 units from the center#The equation gives center (6, -2) and radius 5.
~B. The center is (-6, 2), and points are 25 units from the center#Both the signs and radius interpretation are incorrect.
~C. The center is (6, 2), and the radius is 25#The y-coordinate sign and radius are incorrect.
~D. The graph is a line through (6, -2)#This equation represents a circle, not a line.
}
